=You are a personal financial planning assistant reviewing a Monte Carlo stress test run against a user's real linked accounts.

Current Baseline:
Net Worth: ${{ $json.netWorth }}
Monthly Income: ${{ $json.monthlyIncome }}
Monthly Expenses: ${{ $json.monthlyExpenses }}
Emergency Fund Coverage: {{ $json.emergencyFundMonths }} months

Life Event Tested: {{ $json.lifeEvent.type }}
Home Price: {{ $json.lifeEvent.homePrice }} | Down Payment: {{ $json.lifeEvent.downPaymentPct }}% | Job Loss Duration: {{ $json.lifeEvent.jobLossDurationMonths }} months
Simulation Horizon: {{ $json.horizonMonths }} months across {{ $json.simulation.iterations }} Monte Carlo runs

Simulated Outcomes:
P10 (pessimistic) ending net worth: ${{ $json.simulation.p10EndingNetWorth }}
P50 (median) ending net worth: ${{ $json.simulation.p50EndingNetWorth }}
P90 (optimistic) ending net worth: ${{ $json.simulation.p90EndingNetWorth }}
Probability of running out of money at some point: {{ $json.simulation.shortfallProbabilityPct }}%

Write a plain-English narrative a non-expert could understand, assign one risk level, and give three short, concrete, actionable recommendations.

Return a JSON object with EXACTLY these fields:
{
  "narrativeSummary": "3-4 sentences, plain English, specific to these numbers",
  "riskLevel": "One of: Low, Moderate, High, Severe",
  "topRecommendations": ["short actionable recommendation 1", "short actionable recommendation 2", "short actionable recommendation 3"]
}

Return ONLY the JSON. No markdown, no explanation.

parameters.jsCode:
const item = $input.item.json;
let parsed = {};
try {
  const rawText = item.output || item.text || item.response || '{}';
  const clean = rawText.replace(/```json|```/g, '').trim();
  parsed = JSON.parse(clean);
} catch (e) {
  const pct = item.simulation.shortfallProbabilityPct;
  const risk = pct >= 25 ? 'Severe' : pct >= 10 ? 'High' : pct >= 3 ? 'Moderate' : 'Low';
  parsed = {
    narrativeSummary: `Simulating ${item.lifeEvent.type} over ${item.horizonMonths} months against your real accounts shows a ${pct}% chance your balance goes negative at some point. Median ending net worth is $${item.simulation.p50EndingNetWorth}.`,
    riskLevel: risk,
    topRecommendations: [
      'Build a larger cash buffer before taking on this life event.',
      'Reassess monthly expenses to create more breathing room.',
      'Re-run this simulation after adjusting the event parameters.'
    ]
  };
}

const reportGeneratedAt = new Date().toISOString();
const recommendationsText = (parsed.topRecommendations || [])
  .map((r, i) => `${i + 1}. ${r}`)
  .join('\n');

const reportBody = `Personal Financial Digital Twin Report\nGenerated: ${reportGeneratedAt}\n\nLife Event Tested: ${item.lifeEvent.type}\nSimulation Horizon: ${item.horizonMonths} months (${item.simulation.iterations} Monte Carlo runs)\n\nCurrent Net Worth: $${item.netWorth}\nMonthly Income: $${item.monthlyIncome} | Monthly Expenses: $${item.monthlyExpenses}\nEmergency Fund Coverage: ${item.emergencyFundMonths} months\n\nSimulated Outcomes:\n- Pessimistic (P10) Ending Net Worth: $${item.simulation.p10EndingNetWorth}\n- Median (P50) Ending Net Worth: $${item.simulation.p50EndingNetWorth}\n- Optimistic (P90) Ending Net Worth: $${item.simulation.p90EndingNetWorth}\n- Probability of Running Out of Money: ${item.simulation.shortfallProbabilityPct}%\n\nRisk Level: ${parsed.riskLevel}\n\n${parsed.narrativeSummary}\n\nRecommendations:\n${recommendationsText}`;

return {
  json: {
    ...item,
    ...parsed,
    reportGeneratedAt,
    reportBody
  }
};
